// A PHANTOM type parameter, and this fixture exists because v0.0.272 shipped without it.
//
// `Tagged<T>` never uses `T` in a field, so `Tagged<String>` is one Int in the layout and cannot
// dangle — reading it out of a region-built parent is a copy. The first version of B36's gate asked
// the type ARGUMENTS before asking the declaration, so `String` as an argument condemned a type that
// stores no String, and stage-1 refused a program stage-0 accepts.
//
// The loop was redundant as well as wrong: substitution binds the arguments and the fields are read
// THROUGH them, so an argument that reaches storage already shows up as a field. An argument that
// reaches no field is exactly the phantom case, and asking about it is a false refusal.
//
// Stage-0 arrives at the same answer by a different road — it never sees a generic application at
// this site, because the type is already monomorphised to `Tagged$String` and its `Named` path runs.
// Two roads, one answer, and the fixture pins the answer rather than either road.
class Tagged<T> { n: Int }
class Outer { inner: Tagged<String>, s: String }
let mutable kept: Tagged<String> = Tagged { n: 0 };
region r {
    let o: Outer = Outer { inner: Tagged { n: 42 }, s: "a" + "b" };
    kept = o.inner;
}
print(kept.n);
